{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>For it is the life of all flesh — its blood is in its life</b> — Its blood is to it in place of the life, for the life depends upon it — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 17:14). But it is not correct. Rather, it is possible to interpret \"בְנַפְשׁוֹ\" here as \"in its body,\" so that it says: the life of all flesh, its blood is in its body. And similarly: \"irons came into his soul\" (Psalms 105:18), \"seventy souls\" (Exodus 1:5), \"he shall not come upon the soul of one who is dead\" (Numbers 6:6) — meaning the body, for a body that possesses a soul is called נֶפֶשׁ. \\n\\nAnd in my opinion, Scripture mentioned three things concerning blood, and they are one meaning: first it said (Leviticus 17:11), \"for the life of the flesh is in the blood\"; then it reversed and said, \"for the life of all flesh, its blood is in its life,\" to say that the blood is the life and the life is in the blood, the two being intermingled together — and this is like wine mixed with water, where the water is in the wine and the wine is in the water, each one within the other. And afterward it explained, \"for the blood is the life\" itself, to say that they became one entity and do not separate: no blood is found without life and no life without blood, as is known concerning the spirit, whose origin is from the heart, which is the hyle for all the spirits, and from it comes that which generates the blood, and the blood is that which brings it into being and sustains it — like the relationship of matter and form in all corporeal beings, such that the one is not found without the other. \\n\\nAnd our Sages expounded (Keritot 4a) that there are three excisions (כְּרֵיתוֹת) in the blood: one for the blood of the life, one for the blood of slaughter, and one for the blood of covering. And this is their reason for the excisions, but the reason for the variation in expression concerning the life and the blood is as I have explained."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 17, :notes {0 "Reading הִיּוּלִי as the Hebrew transliteration of the philosophical term 'hyle' (matter/prime matter), and גֹּלֶם וְהַצּוּרָה as 'matter and form' (גֹּלֶם = hylē, צוּרָה = morphē), standard medieval philosophical paired terms."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:54:59.111645301Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}